Date: Thu, 9 Dec 1993 13:02:51 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: GENDER: Testing the Limits

I think there are male behaviors which turn out to be useful in many
modern fields:

- territoriality...staking out a memetic claim on some turf

- aggressiveness...taking a stand, an opinion

- testing limits (see territoriality)

There are reports (sorry, as Curtis Frye has so eloquently--Welcome,
Curtis!--put it, these are informal debates....I left my index cards
in high school) that little girls playing with computers and video
games are _afraid they'll break the computer_. By contrast, little
boys apparently _hope_ to break it!

(Not out of pure malice, though that may be a factor, but out of
pushing the limits of what they can do, how they can conquer the
system, and possibly how they can defeat the overall heierachical
system.)

It turns out that this desire to "break" the system is very useful for
learning new fields. No better way to learn a computer system or
language than to "break" it, to push the envelope, to undertstand the
behavior of the system at the boundaries. To test the limits.

And of course "nuking the old paradigm" as Einstein, Planck, Darwin,
Newton, and on and on did is a wonderful way to break the system.
Those who are afraid of disturbing the order, who are anxious to
please, etc., are not likely to have this effect.

Of course, this is not the whole story, nor am I saying there's no
place for different perspectives. Time will tell. So far, the
"feminine" apprach to science and technology has not resulted in
anything really significant, just reinterpretations.

(Digression: a well-known proponent--it may be Mary Ann Keller-- of
the "feminine view of science" has recast the whole Newtonian
model. Gone are the "forces" that "push" things around, as these are
"male" concepts that are "off-putting" to women and other sensitive
beings. Replaced by "envelopings" and "coming togethers." The Sun, it
seems, does not "pull" and "push" on the planets. Rather, they
participate in a "process" of mutual understanding and an
all-enveloping sense of unfolding and peace.)

In line with what Amara has said about not having been taught this
kind of assertiveness (related ideas: "bulshitting," "SWAGing," quick
estimates, thinking on one's feet, jumping in to make points, staking
out memetic territory, etc.), this is where our educational system,
and perhaps underlying basic gender differences, ill-prepare most
women for the rough-and-tumble of life in technological enterprises.

"The Samson Syndrome" -- I claim that the _true scientist_ does not
want _order_ and _neatness_....rather, he wants to see the while
edifice fall!

Nothing pumps the adrenaline faster--well, one or two other things
do--than thinking one is "on" to a cosmic secret, in math or physics
or whatever, that will shake things up and recast all of science in a
different light. I felt this raw sense of power in 1977 when I figured
out what was causing the bits to flip in our memory chips (alpha
particles, cosmic rays) and for several months I had an amazing sense
of realization that I was the first person on the Earth to know this
and that I could then "see" what the future would bring for chips and
memories and error correction systems.

Ten years later, in 1987, I realized the long-term implications of
strong crypto for achieving anarcho-capitalist goals, and I had a
similar sense of power, of awe, and of sheer pleasure at the thought
that _my ideas_ (which I call "crypto anarchy") could cause the whole
system to come crumbling down!

My guess is that far fewer women than men think in this aggressive,
territorial, boundary-testing, destructive, and Samsonian way.

--Tim May


