On Wed, 8 Dec 1993, Ray wrote:
> > > We will for _sure_ run out of IP addresses with this thing on the
> > > street.
> 
>   Correct me if I'm wrong, but the IP address is a 32-bit number
> (4 octets) This means there are 2^32 possible IP addresses (minus
> 127.0 and some of the other subnets). This means billions of computers
> must have net addresses. I find this unlikely anytime soon.
> 

In theory yes, there should be enough. But there are some problems. Since
there are 3 basic types of IP addresses, I'll just use the Class C address
as an example. This has 8 bits for the host id and 21 bits for the net id.
Most networks that require more than 255 hosts will use the large Class B
addressing initially to avoid needing another net id. But there are still
many networks with small numbers of hosts. This means the other IP addresses
which are unused are effectively wasted (say I only have 5 or 6 machines
on my local network. If I have a Class C address, I'm 'wasting' the other
250 addresses)

I may be simplifying things a bit, and I am probably ignoring many things,
but generally to use these unused addresses would require a massive kludge
to the current routing systems in use...

Ethernet uses 48-bit physical addresses which works much better since,
although interface boards must have unique addresses, they don't need them
organized for routing in the same way IP addresses need. I don't recall
what the OSI protocol's suggest for Internet addresses...

