If you claim that the sole cause of violence is addictive drugs,  then
I want to see the evidence that these mass murderers were addicted to
heroin.  I haven't heard anything about the train/post office killers,
etc., being addicted.  It's a deeper problem than that,  although I
agree that heroin addiction is a human tragedy.

Well, I think it's self-evident that it's a problem with ethics and values.
But I see my environment becoming incrementally more dangerous,  and I'd like
to know what to do about it other than study martial arts (which I have)
and pack heat (which I have not).

On the way to work today I rode in the same subway car with three black
teenagers who were loudly and enthusiastically talking about a fight that
one of them was in,  in which he got his opponent down on the ground and
kicked him repeatedly in the head.  I felt very sad because I didn't feel
that any of these kids were evil at all,  they were not much different
than I was at that age in most respects.  Bright-eyed, smart, happy to
be alive, full of hope for the future.  Yet violent.  Given the statistics
for murder and incarceration for black youths today,  the cards are stacked
against them.

This is a violent culture.  I grew up in a small town in Michigan.  Until 
I was old enough to know how to avoid them,  I was constantly in fights 
when I was a young kid,  never started by myself.  When there was a fight,
all of the other kids would stand around in a circle and egg the participants 
on.  Even the teachers would wait for a while before breaking fights up,
if the fight was at school, which was often the case.  The local obsession,
football, further encouraged respect for physical aggression.

What is the difference between the kids on the subway and the ones I grew
up with?  Only the level of violence that is socially acceptable.  Instead
of it being OK to start a fight as long as you didn't use weapons like where
I grew up,  it's acceptable in Cambridge Mass. to kick someone in the head,
or to use knives and guns.  People behave according to the expectations for
their behavior in their environment.

The problem with decrying the loss of ethics and values is that it does
nothing to solve the problem.  Violent children grow up to be even more 
violent adults.  The culture of violence needs to be replaced with a
culture of tolerance and respect for others.

Here are a number of things that could be done:

1. Violence should not be tolerated in schools.  Students who commit
   violent acts in school should be arrested, prosecuted and punished.
   Schools should be privatized to encourage competition and end the
   mediocrity that passes for education.

2. The legal distinction between minors and adults needs to be eliminated.
   Do an adult crime, get an adult punishment.  The coddling of violent
   kids by the system needs to end now.

3. Law enforcement is a lousy way to attack the problem.  What we need
   is crime prevention.  Crime prevention services should be marketed
   to violent cities,  who would be paid based on the reduction in
   crime rates.  They would do things like: patrol neighborhoods, help
   people with setting up alarm systems,  martial arts and weapons
   training,  assist prosecution in the manner of private detectives,
   etc.  They should be private businesses, not government agencies.

4. Self-defense should be taught in schools.

5. The war on drugs must end to allow resources to be used where they
   are really needed.

Andy


