I believe that postpartum depression results from certain misguided
birthing practices and not the experience of birth itself.  At the time
that my children were born (17-19 years ago) standard hospital procedure
was to incapacitate the mother with drugs, stirrups, and sometimes even
straps, exclude the father from the birthing room, forcibly extract the
child from the dazed mother and then remove it to a nursery until the
mother came around.  I'd be depressed too.  Our children were born at home
and my wife's postpartum condition both times was one of extended rapture.

The birth experience is *not* always one of pain and suffering.  It is true
that many things can go wrong in the birthing process to produce pain.  I
am also aware that some women resent the use of the word "discomfort"
rather than "pain" by teachers of the Lamaze natural birth method. 
However, pregnancy is not a disease and childbirth is not a medical trauma,
although it is a physiological event of mind-boggling proportion.  My wife
found the sensations of labor and childbearing to be intense and at times
very uncomfortable, but to be the expected attendants of a powerful and
natural transformation which she (and I) would not have missed.

It *is* possible to raise children who are considerate, responsible,
independent, productive and thoughtful.  I have two of them.  And yes one
of them even had a ring through her nose for some time, although she has
always paid for her own drugs-of-choice.  

