   One extropian idea essential to music (and art) is applied mathemathics.
   Bach (I think) used simple number sequences and well defined permutaions to
   produce harmonic and quite good sounding tunes. I don't remeber exact
   details, but I will find out more about this.

One of my favorite topics!

Mozart had a waltz permutation algorithm that used dice to write waltzes,  
which was later incorporated into a work called HPSCHD by John Cage and 
Lejaren Hiller in the 50s.

Here are some more recent examples of the explicit use of mathematics in
writing music:

Fibonacci Series: Bela Bartok, Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta

Group Theory: Iannis Xenakis, Nomos Alpha

Probability Theory: Iannis Xenakis, Pithoprakta

Game Theory: Xenakis, Strategie for two orchestras

Fractals: Gyorgy Ligeti, Piano concerto

Books to check out:

Joseph Schillinger, The Mathematical Basis for the Arts
Joseph Schillinger, The Schillinger System of Musical Composition

-Schillinger's work was written in the early 20th century and is
 a kind of touchstone on this topic

Iannis Xenakis, Formalized Music
N. Matossian, Xenakis (biography)
 (there are english and french editions of both of these)

-Xenakis, an engineer and architect who became a composer, is a real
 pioneer in applying mathematics to composition.

