Date: Thu, 9 Dec 93 19:03:09 EST
From: Andy Wilson <ajw@Think.COM>
Subject: POLITICS: The Polly Klaas Bill

   From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
   Date: Thu, 9 Dec 1993 15:47:29 +0000 (GMT)

   Andy Wilson <ajw@Think.COM> writes:

   >Prisons should cost the taxpayers nothing.  They should turn a profit.
					     ^
   Up to the caret, I agree with you. But I'd be VIOLENTLY opposed to the
   second sentence. Reason? Well ...

   >Just because someone's in jail doesn't entitle them to free food, shelter,
   >entertainment and smokes.  They should work just like the rest of us.
   >I object to capital punishment,  but in China, they make the executee's 
   >family pay for the bullets.  No reason we can't as well.

   In China, they have concentration camp factories that make a profit by
   exporting cheap goods.  Life expectancy in such camps is short; there's
   no concern for safety, working conditions are as bad as anything seen in
   the old Stalinist Gulags or the Nazi concentration camps, and there is
   an emphasis on productivity rather than punishment or rehabilitation.
   Simply put, the prisons are used as cheap sources of sweated labour.  It
   has been said that if you're a poor Chinese you can wind up in such a
   camp simply because the commandant needs more slaves.  Consider: once a
   prison becomes a profit center, there is a positive incentive for a
   government to lock people away.  This has gone so far that it is now
   estimated that there are 20-40 _million_ people in such camps.  Indeed,
   they're one of the major forex-earners for the Chinese government.

I agree that the Chinese camps are apalling.

Why not let the prisoners choose which prison they go to?  Then they would
go to the one that treats them the best, but is still able to turn a
profit.  Cruel prisons would go out of business.  In addition, sensible
people would boycott the products of inhumane prisons.  Also, anyone should
be able to visit a prison and see what it is like.

I don't believe prisons should be harsh,  they should just keep the
prisoners in.  As long as they are in prison and pay their own way,
I don't care how well off they are as long as certain basic human dignity
conditions are met,  that are not at all met by our current prisons that
are like medieval nightmares full of violence, rape, murder, and drugs.

Andy

